WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.670
The overall score combines
quiet hours and busy commutes.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.410
To inspect that mixture, define
exactly which rows belong to a slice.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.950
Rush clock hours here are seven,
eight and nine in the morning,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.430
and sixteen,
seventeen and eighteen in the afternoon.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.290
This window exists on every day.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.130
Working day rush adds the requirement
that the working day flag is one.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.490
There are one thousand and ninety
two rush clock observations,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.230
but only seven hundred and fifty
working day rush observations.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.790
Mixing those definitions would attach
the right number to the wrong population.

00:00:40.192 --> 00:00:45.672
The seven hundred and fifty working day
rush observations have mean absolute error

00:00:45.672 --> 00:00:48.342
one hundred and seventy
eight point one nine.

00:00:49.112 --> 00:00:50.148
All other three thousand,

00:00:50.148 --> 00:00:54.622
six hundred and seven observations
average seventy three point five six.

00:00:55.312 --> 00:01:00.002
Multiply each mean by its observation
count, add those error totals,

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:02.382
and divide by four thousand,
three hundred and fifty seven.

00:01:03.492 --> 00:01:07.682
This recovers the overall ninety
one point five seven score.

00:01:08.212 --> 00:01:13.432
Simply averaging the two means would give
the small rush population equal weight to

00:01:13.432 --> 00:01:14.982
the much larger remainder.

00:01:15.752 --> 00:01:19.562
The count tells us which objective
the aggregate actually measures.

00:01:21.775 --> 00:01:27.015
The implementation selects a slice by row
indices and passes the same indices to

00:01:27.015 --> 00:01:27.585
predictions.

00:01:28.255 --> 00:01:33.445
It retains count, absolute error,
squared error, residual, deficit

00:01:33.955 --> 00:01:37.205
and excess totals,
rather than only rounded means.

00:01:37.995 --> 00:01:42.195
Each dimension must reconstruct the
original population and those error

00:01:42.195 --> 00:01:42.845
totals.

00:01:43.695 --> 00:01:48.175
The independent audit verifies six hundred
and eighty six slice summaries across

00:01:48.175 --> 00:01:51.405
seven model policy pairs
and both partitions.

00:01:52.235 --> 00:01:57.345
Hour, month and day type each describe
the same underlying observations.

00:01:58.275 --> 00:02:02.345
Adding counts across these overlapping
dimensions would count hours repeatedly.

00:02:03.255 --> 00:02:04.805
Check each partition on its own.

00:02:06.998 --> 00:02:12.338
January contains seven hundred and forty
recorded validation hours and has mean

00:02:12.338 --> 00:02:14.988
absolute error thirty nine point six one.

00:02:15.738 --> 00:02:19.908
June contains seven hundred and twenty
and has error one hundred and forty three.

00:02:21.038 --> 00:02:24.968
Mean signed error also becomes
much more negative by June.

00:02:25.658 --> 00:02:28.168
These differences are hidden
by the single aggregate.

00:02:28.538 --> 00:02:32.178
July through December contain
zero validation observations,

00:02:32.178 --> 00:02:35.928
because those dates belong
outside the retained window.

00:02:36.438 --> 00:02:39.848
We do not borrow reserved test
outcomes to fill the table.

00:02:40.198 --> 00:02:44.958
A complete category domain can contain
empty positions while the actual evidence

00:02:44.958 --> 00:02:47.328
remains limited to its observed dates.

00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:52.733
Season names need the
same coverage discipline.

00:02:53.203 --> 00:02:56.033
Winter here means January and February.

00:02:56.683 --> 00:03:01.073
Spring means March through May,
while summer means only June.

00:03:01.843 --> 00:03:04.033
Autumn has no observations at all.

00:03:04.503 --> 00:03:07.413
Its metric is undefined
and stored as null.

00:03:08.003 --> 00:03:10.053
If we displayed zero instead,

00:03:10.443 --> 00:03:14.393
the figure would misleadingly suggest
perfectly predicted autumn hours.

00:03:14.923 --> 00:03:17.393
The chart explicitly labels no score.

00:03:18.263 --> 00:03:22.293
For root mean squared error,
another denominator issue appears:

00:03:23.023 --> 00:03:26.673
first combine squared error totals,
divide by count,

00:03:27.043 --> 00:03:28.433
then take the square root.

00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:33.503
Averaging seasonal root mean squared
errors generally does not reproduce the

00:03:33.503 --> 00:03:34.573
overall value.

00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:40.117
We also group errors by the
eventual observed count.

00:03:40.607 --> 00:03:44.097
Hours below fifty rentals
average error near twelve,

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:47.897
while hours at least two hundred average
about one hundred and sixty eight.

00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:53.667
That is useful retrospective evidence that
the high count part of the population is

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:54.217
difficult.

00:03:54.787 --> 00:03:58.857
Now consider using that band to choose
a model when issuing a forecast.

00:03:59.427 --> 00:04:02.257
We cannot know the band
without knowing the outcome.

00:04:02.727 --> 00:04:06.807
Turning this diagnostic into a routing
input would leak the target into

00:04:06.807 --> 00:04:07.457
prediction.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:11.157
The slice remains an
explanation of saved results,

00:04:11.447 --> 00:04:15.137
alongside its actual count and
explicit retrospective scope.

